{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfbew3SQkmdj2AyUgeB39", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7302, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7302}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "4861986e-ff9b-4615-b2c7-8e29a8bf993e", "uuid": "ad94c4e2-7b6b-430d-9c03-78ed275ce6b5", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T14:13:04.553Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfbew3BmsAoeC3qLGUHv8"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfbew3SQkmdj2AyUgeB39", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Banker's rounding** (also called round-half-to-even, or ROUND_HALF_EVEN) is a rule for values that fall exactly halfway between two candidates. Instead of always rounding the half up, you round to the nearest **even** digit.\n\n| Value | Round half up | Banker's |\n|-------|--------------|----------|\n| 0.5 \u2192 | 1 | 0 |\n| 1.5 \u2192 | 2 | 2 |\n| 2.5 \u2192 | 3 | 2 |\n| 3.5 \u2192 | 4 | 4 |\n\nAnything not exactly halfway rounds normally (2.51 \u2192 3, 2.49 \u2192 2).\n\n**Why accountants like it**\n\n- **It removes systematic bias.** Round-half-up always nudges ties in the same direction. Across thousands or millions of entries, such as interest calculations, tax lines or currency conversions, that adds up to a drift. With round-half-even, ties go up about half the time and down about half the time, so the errors largely cancel.\n- **Totals stay closer to the truth.** The sum of the rounded values tracks the sum of the unrounded values better. This matters when line items must reconcile with a grand total.\n- **It is a standard.** It is the default rounding mode in IEEE 754 floating point. It is also the default in many financial libraries and in some accounting and tax rules, for example .NET's `Math.Round` and Python's `round()`.\n\n**Caveats**\n\n- The effect only appears with exact ties. With arbitrary decimals, ties are rare, so the bias matters mostly for data with a fixed number of decimal places, such as cents.\n- Some jurisdictions or contracts specify round-half-up, so check which rule is required.\n- Use decimal types, not binary floats, for money. A value like 2.675 isn't exactly representable as a float, so it may not be a true tie.\n\nThis repo has a stored decision that tax uses ROUND_HALF_EVEN, so this applies directly to the code here."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7302, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7302}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "4861986e-ff9b-4615-b2c7-8e29a8bf993e", "uuid": "0045ca95-655c-46c3-8e73-2a508eb04bad", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T14:13:09.668Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfbew3BmsAoeC3qLGUHv8"}
